ty by occupation/age. (d) Early growth\\u2014estimate behavioral Rb in early window and compare. (e) Inequality\\u2014compute Gini of adoption across age_group/occupation. Perform sensitivity analyses on enforcement parameters to assess robustness of \\u653f\\u7b56\\u4e0d\\u5408\\u89c4\\u7387 and \\u5904\\u7f5a\\u6b21\\u6570/\\u4eba\\u5747\\u5904\\u7f5a.",\n    "key_variables_to_calibrate": [\n      "baseline_adoption_intercept_alpha0",\n      "neighbor_influence_betas (\\u03b2_f, \\u03b2_w, \\u03b2_c)",\n      "risk_perception_weight_gamma",\n      "habit_persistence_phi",\n      "relapse_rate_sigma_off and adoption_rate_sigma_on",\n      "effect_lag_L",\n      "layer_contact_frequency_weights",\n      "info_conversion_kappa and exogenous_info_rate_lambda",\n      "triadic_closure_probability",\n      "noncompliance_probability_p_nc and mandate_effect_delta"\n    ]\n  },\n  "file_summaries": [\n    "- Data structure and type:\\n  - CSV; one row per agent (flat, tabular). No nested elements or explicit edges. Suitable as the node/agent attribute table for a multi-layer social network.\\n\\n- Column meanings:\\n  - agent_id: unique agent identifier (primary key; for joins with edge lists if provided).\\n  - age: integer age in years.\\n  - age_group: categorical bucket derived from age (e.g., Youth, Young Adult, Middle Age).\\n  - occupation: categorical role (e.g., Student, Blue Collar, White Collar); implies presence/strength of work/school layer ties.\\n  - risk_perception: float in [0,1]; baseline propensity to adopt mask-wearing and to be influenced by risk cues.\\n  - initial_mask_wearing: boolean; agent\\u2019s mask state at t0.\\n  - family_connections: integer degree (number of ties) in the family/household layer.\\n  - work_school_connections: integer degree in the work/school layer.\\n  - community_connections: integer degree in the broader community layer.\\n  - total_connections: integer; derived = family + work/school + community (use for validation; redundant computationally).\\n\\n- Relationships or nested elements:\\n  - No nested structures; relationships are implied via layer-specific degree counts, not explicit adjacency. total_connections should equal the sum of layer degrees.\\n  - age_group is functionally dependent on age; total_connections is functionally dependent on the three layer degrees.\\n\\n- How this informs simulation entities/interactions:\\n  - Agents: instantiate one agent per row with attributes {age, age_group, occupation, risk_perception, mask_state}.\\n  - Network: generate a multi-layer graph (family, work/school, community) using the provided degree sequence per layer (e.g., configuration model per layer), ensuring undirected/symmetric ties and matching degree totals across agents.\\n  - Influence dynamics: compute mask-wearing adoption/cessation probabilities as functions of risk_perception and the fraction of masked neighbors per layer, with optional layer weights (e.g., family > work/school > community) and occupation/age modifiers (e.g., Students emphasize work/school contacts).\\n  - Initialization: set mask state from initial_mask_wearing; validate degree sums; optionally reconcile age_group with age.\\n  - Metrics: total_connections can guide contact frequency or exposure weighting across layers.",\n    "Semantic metadata summary for social_network.json\\n\\n- Overall structure and type\\n  - JSON object (dictionary) mapping agent IDs (as strings) to adjacency lists grouped by social context.\\n  - Each value is a nested object with four keys: family, work_school, community, all. Each key maps to a list of integer IDs representing neighbors.\\n\\n- Meaning of keys\\n  - family: Household/kin ties; strongest, frequent, repeated contacts.\\n  - work_school: Coworkers or classmates; medium-strength, routine contacts.\\n  - community: Broader social contacts (neighbors, friends, activities); weaker, less frequent.\\n  - all: Union of all contacts across the three contexts; redundant